.nh
.TH "TUSK" "1" "Jul 2026" "Tusk" "Tusk Manual"

.SH NAME
tusk-node-get - Print the source file (markdown or HTML) for a node by id


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtusk node get  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the source file (frontmatter/markup + body) for a node by id.

.PP
The node id is the workspace-relative path: markdown nodes drop the
extension (notes/hello.md has id "notes/hello"), while HTML nodes retain
it (page.html has id "page.html").

.PP
By default (no flags) the command prints the raw file to stdout
verbatim — useful for piping into editors, less, or another tusk command.
When --include, --fields, --format, or --json is passed the command emits
structured output instead (compact for TTY, JSON otherwise). --fields keeps
only the named fields, as on tusk query: properties. keeps one
property, and an unknown name is an error.

.PP
--include paths lists the workspace paths the page names (in inline code,
links, or a paths-only frontmatter value) under edge types with
paths = true, each with its lines and a live exists flag, the way
tusk query --include paths does. It is never part of the default shape.

.PP
--include edges lists the node's edges in both directions, the way
tusk edge list shows them: a link another file writes in a paragraph
reads as one edge from that file, with units naming the passages that
hold it, and the node's contains edges to its own sections and
paragraphs are left out. --edge-type and --direction in|out keep one
edge type or one direction, and --sub-units lists the stored rows as
they are. Each of the three implies --include edges.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--direction\fP=""
	keep only in or out edges (implies --include edges)

.PP
\fB--edge-type\fP=""
	keep only edges of this type (implies --include edges)

.PP
\fB--fields\fP=[]
	keep only these fields (comma-separated; properties. for one property)

.PP
\fB--format\fP=""
	output format: compact|json (default: compact for TTY, json otherwise)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for get

.PP
\fB--include\fP=[]
	expand returned shape: body|edges|properties|paths (comma-separated; paths lists the workspace paths the page names)

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	emit structured JSON (sugar for --format json)

.PP
\fB--sub-units\fP[=false]
	list sub-unit edges as stored instead of folded onto their file (implies --include edges)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	emit debug-level logs to stderr

.PP
\fB-C\fP, \fB--workspace\fP=""
	run as if started in \fBdir\fR: find tusk.toml from there instead of the working directory (default $TUSK_WORKSPACE)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  # Print the raw file
  tusk node get notes/hello

  # Structured JSON envelope with only the body
  tusk node get notes/hello --include body --format json

  # The workspace paths a page names
  tusk node get technical/ledger --include paths

  # Only the files that link to a page
  tusk node get notes/hello --edge-type references --direction in

  # Open in $EDITOR (round-trip through a temp file)
  tusk node get notes/hello > /tmp/hello.md && $EDITOR /tmp/hello.md
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtusk-node(1)\fP
